        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p39-q32-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "T=\\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p39-q32-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use the cosine rule to find ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\sin C",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " exactly, apply the area rule and use part (a) with ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "2s=a+b+c",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Let $T$ be the area and let $P$ be the product in part (a). The cosine rule gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos C&=\\frac{a^2+b^2-c^2}{2ab}.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $0^\\circ<C<180^\\circ$, $\\sin C>0$. Using $\\sin^2C+\\cos^2C=1$ and part (a),\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin C&=\\sqrt{1-\\left(\\frac{a^2+b^2-c^2}{2ab}\\right)^2}\\\\\\sin C&=\\frac{\\sqrt{P}}{2ab}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe triangle area rule gives\n$$\\begin{align*}T&=\\frac12ab\\sin C\\\\T&=\\frac14\\sqrt{P}.\\end{align*}$$\nBecause $s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}$, the four factors of $P$ are $2s$, $2(s-c)$, $2(s-b)$ and $2(s-a)$:\n$$\\begin{align*}P&=(2s)\\big(2(s-c)\\big)\\big(2(s-b)\\big)\\big(2(s-a)\\big)\\\\P&=16s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c).\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}T&=\\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 116
